title: 'pallet-revive: deny reentrancy and storage writes from transfer and send receivers'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A contract called through Solidity's `transfer()` or `send()` can no longer call back into
    the sender. The same holds for any call with that shape: value with a gas limit of 0, or no
    value with a gas limit of exactly 2300. On the EVM this already held for zero-value transfers
    and now also applies when value moves, while on PolkaVM both are new behavior. How the denied
    call fails depends on the VM the receiver runs on:
    - EVM: The reentrant call returns 0 to the receiver; before #12673 it ran out of gas, which
      reverted the transfer.
    - PolkaVM: The reentrant call aborts the receiver, so the transfer reverts. Before, the
      receiver ran out of gas while making the reentrant call, with the same result.

    The receiver of such a call, and any contract it calls or creates, can no longer write
    persistent contract storage, even if the slot is warm. On Ethereum such a receiver gets at most
    2300 gas, and EIP-2200 fails any `SSTORE` when 2300 gas or less is left. The write fails with
    `OutOfGas`, as it would on Ethereum. This covers `SSTORE`, the PolkaVM storage host functions,
    and precompiles that write contract storage, such as `clearStorage` and `takeStorage` of the
    storage precompile. Transient storage is not affected. Calls with any other explicit gas limit
    are not checked, even one below 2300.

    An EVM `DELEGATECALL` with a gas limit of exactly 2300 no longer gets the call stipend, aligning
    it with PolkaVM and Ethereum.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Runtime precompiles that write contract storage through `PrecompileExt::set_storage` get an
    `OutOfGas` error in the receiver of a `transfer()`, a `send()` or any call with that shape, and
    in any contract such a receiver calls or creates.
    The new `PrecompileExt::ensure_storage_write_allowed` lets a precompile verify this before a
    write. `PrecompileExt::call` takes a `CallProtections` argument in place of the reentrancy
    protection. It carries the reentrancy protection and `denies_storage_writes`, which stops the
    callee and every frame it creates from writing persistent contract storage.

    `integrity_test` checks that a block cannot write more contract storage than a validator has
    memory for. It scaled a write's weight by the memory that write uses before dividing the block
    weight by it, so the storage term came out near zero and the check could not fail on it. It now
    computes how many writes fit the block weight before multiplying by the memory each one uses.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: patch
